e on the black sea however  in their advance the troops of peter the  great and later catherine ii were  overwhelmed by the unusual resistance of  the caucasian people of course  insurrections didn't lack that of  shaikhman surushurma in the late 1700s  or that of imam shamil in the mid-1800s  were just two of many examples the  resistance of the chechens and the  peoples of the caucasus was based on  three common elements islam the desire  for independence and the similarity of  languages that allowed the caucasian  peoples to communicate with each other  and fear much closer than the slavic  foreigners the russian writer tolstoy  knew kokosus very well and he himself as  reported in his book chad murat greatly  admired the chechens for their fierce  determination in resisting that star  forces not even the annexation to the  russian empire in 1873 quenched their  thirst for independence indeed even  under the soviet regime the caucasian's  resistance showed up several times in  violent riots over two decades between  1917 and 1937  up to the point that for three years  chechnya dagestan and ingoshazia became  independent from russian control stalin  who in the meantime had come to power  didn't like all that turmoil and broads  by forced the three republics under his  control being himself a georgian stalin  knew very well the mechanisms that  animated caucuses the strategy that he  adopted to unhinge this mechanism was  the usual one from the time of the  romans divide and rule let's make  another example of this tactic the  krachais and the balkars people speak  the same language a language of turkish  origin stalin created on the one hand  the socialist republic of karachi  circassia where the karachis were moved  along with the circassian people who  don't speak a turkish language but a  caucasian one and on the other hand he  created the socialist republic of  cabardino balkaria where he sorted the  other turkish people the bulkers along  with the cowboys and other people  speaking a caucasian language these  divisions ended up crushing the culture  and tribal structure of the various  ethnic groups the same stalin did with  false accusations as in the case of  world war ii under the pretext of  chechen collaboration with the german  army a falsehood that never happened  stalin took the opportunity to deport in  a single night in february 44 almost  half a million chechens to siberia and  kazakhstan name of the operation lentil  about hundred thousand chechens died  during the deportation only 1957 under  khrushchev chechens would be allowed to  return to their homeland in the  autonomous soviet socialist republic of  chechnya in gucettia if this all sounds  to you like a big mess it is  nevertheless the majority of the  population in the republic this time the  russian-speaking one that had come after  stalin's deportation caused great  problems for all those chechens who had  returned from siberia but since we don't  want this video to last for ages let's  just go back to the year before i come  to experience this beautiful world  1991  with the end of the ussr all former  soviet republics chose the path of  independence of course despite the  referendum on its preservation that had  clearly shown that 77 percent of soviets  were still in favor of maintaining the  union the autonomous republics of the  caucasus were also faced with a choice  independence or to stay attached with  mother russia the only one to follow the  idea of independence was from the  beginning chechnya ingushetia an old  general the soviet army jahar dutyev who  was elected president of the republic in  1991 pushed it in that direction with 13  years of exile in kazakhstan dudayev had  become the first chechen to become  general of the soviet air force  appointed commander of the heavy bomber  division in estonia the detail that  duday was in charge of a division  responsible for nuclear strikes  suggested that soviet authorities  considered his loyalty beyond any  question yet with the breakaway of  estonia 1991 the dayab retired from the  army and returned to chechnya  rediscovering himself as a strongly  nationalist politician taking advantage  of the disintegration of the ussr dudai  ever proclaimed chechnya's independence  there was an alcoholic issue yeltsin  for the russian president and his  administration the stakes in chechnya  and the entire north koreas were very  high and for three reasons first of all  oil in 1991 chechnya was in fact on the  root of the gas and oil pipelines that  connected the caspian sea and the black  sea certainly not a small matter in  economic terms then there was the  geopolitical questions moscow wanted to  avoid any power struggle in its  southernmost provinces this was an  attempt to make the west understand that  despite the collapse of the ussr the  russians era of influencing the  so-called near abroad had remained  unchanged and finally there was the  factor of stability it was necessary to  preserve the legacy of the imperial  tsarist conquest and especially the  soviet legacy of military bases still  present there all of this was done to  maintain a firm grip in a vital buffer  zone between georgia armenia and  azerbaijan therefore according to  russian logic chechen independence was  unacceptable breaking away from chechnya  and returning to the russian federation  was ingushetia which formally broke away  in december 1992 at that point of all  the former autonomous republics only  chechnya was left to challenge the  central power in moscow today was  convinced that the russian wouldn't  waste blood and money which was already  lacking in russia to bring the caucasian  rebels back into the fort however it  would realize to be profoundly mistaken  in the summer of 1994 the political  situation in grozny the chechen capital  worsened  and we called  was opposed by pro-russian  parliamentarians who were supported by  the kremlin through an economic embargo  a strong disinformation campaign and  armed attacks in the capital with the  first signs of chechen resistance on  december 1994 yeltsin was forced to  mobilize the army to restore what he  called the federal order it was the  beginning of the first chechen war a war  that would see one of the harshest  bombings since the one in dresden in  1945 and a war in which the russians  would experience at first hand a famous  caucasian motto that to defeat the  chechen you need another chechen on  paper the russians had all the  advantages on their side starting with  an army of more than 2 million men an  army that however was formed by quite  obsolete means and by soldiers who since  the end of the war in afghanistan hadn't  received an up-to-date training as a  consequence the russian infantry found  itself displaced when faced with a type  of combat to be adopted in chechnya on  one side the high-intensity close combat  in the urban spaces of grozny and on the  other side the lack of effective command  only left room for improvisation not all  of them proved to be incapable of course  among those who stood out were the  spatznets the russian elite corpse and  the vdv the airborne troops the russians  assumed that the capture of grozny would  mean the end of the war but the chechens  anticipated the moves placing three  defensive rings around the city and  turning it into a fortified nest with  land attacks being repelled by the  chechens who had no problems using  civilians as human shields the russians  increased their artillery attacks on the  city eventually devastating it with  bombing raids within a month what  remains of the capital fell into their  hands the fellow grozny was followed by  the flight of chechen soldiers into the  mountains and the discovery of thousands  of bodies estimated to be more than 35  000. the usce would describe that  bloodbath as an unthinkable catastrophe  when in april 1996 the head of the  chechen republic the diet was  intercepted by the russians and killed  with the guided missile the resistance  passed into the hands of his successor  another secessionist general azlan  mashadov the first thing mashadov did  was to launch a second assault on grozny  this time occupied by the russians this  kinda reminds me when a thief enters the  store only to be locked from the outside  by the owner with several thousand  russian soldiers ending up has hostages  of the chechen forces machado managed to  negotiate a temporary peace agreement  signed directly in dagestan in kazaburt  that agreement marked the momentary  victory for the about fifteen thousand  separatists but the balance of the war  was already devastating over one hundred  thousand dead nearly ten percent of the  chechen population while nearly two  hundred thousand of chechens were  refugees in afghanistan in essence in  the span of just two years more than a  third of the chechen population was  either six feet under or displaced the  regional economy was devastated and the  country had become a no man's land and  peace didn't mean friendship and only  served to freeze the church and  sovereignty the russians refused to pay  for war operations as well as for the  revenues from the passage of oil and gas  pipelines on the contrary they started  the project of another oil pipeline to  bypass chechnya altogether the baku  novoroysk pipeline this fluctuating  situation favored the development of two  phenomena in the country the arrival of  radical sunny islam of wahhabi  inspiration and the increase in  organized crime the radicalization was  fostered by the flow of money coming  straight from saudi arabia while the  local underworld was supported by the  contacts that the chechen warlords had  with the russian mafia all of this  generated the dynamite box ready to  explode in three years between 1996 and  1999 more than 1 300 kidnappings of  politicians ambassadors and foreigners  were recorded and things became more  complicated when in the mid 1990s more  chechens who had fled abroad in the  previous decades began to return from  the middle east some of this especially  those from jordan became the links  between locals and middle eastern  salafist extremists these invited saudi  and al-qaeda-affiliated commander emir  katab to chechnya qatab had fought as a  warlord against the soviets in  afghanistan where among others he had  been around bin laden circles in 1995 he  entered chechnya pretending to be a  journalist and began training chechens  by distributing funding and weapons  supplied by al-qaeda through the saudis  katsab for his part made no effort to  conceal his real goal which wasn't  chechan independence but to raise a  general jihad throughout the north  caucasus drive out christian russia and  create an islamic caliphate all it  needed was a spark qatar along with his  independentist colleague shamir basayev  found this park in april 1999 in  neighboring dagestan where a certain  bagudin magomedov proclaimed himself  emir and declared jihad for the  liberation of the caucasus and the birth  of the islamic state of dagestan  [Music]  this pissed off the russians  indeed 1999 russia was no longer the  drunken rush of yeltsin and putin was  the first that needed a higher profile  triumph  chechnya seemed an excellent opportunity  to flex his muscle and empower the  government putin also wanted to  indirectly warn the nato bloc with that  intervention implying one thing that the  caucasus was still a russian affair  however the russian intervention in  dagestan was the pretext for the let us  say controversial explosion of a series  of bombs that tore apart some apartment  buildings in three cities moscow bouinax  and bolgodonsk according to sami could  have been a provocation organized by the  russian security agencies themselves  also because a similar device linked to  the russian secret services the fsb was  found by chance in the city of ryazan  the event was used by the kremlin to  declare war a second time on the  illegitimate chechen government the  support of the russian population  obviously traumatized by the attacks on  their borders was very wide instead of  the reckless frontal assault that had  characterized the first war this time  the russian strategy was based on a well  organized and methodical plan russia  sent 30 000 soldiers to the northern  borders of chechnya starting a  stranglehold from three points north  east and west in a few weeks another  twenty thousand men joined the  sprinter-like maneuver arriving at the  end of october at the gates of grozny  with the difference that this time the  internal areas of the country had been  completely neutralized in the first war  in fact the objective had been  exclusively the capital and to do so the  various warlords had been left in  control of the countryside between mass  graves filled with corpses men suspected  of being rebels and then shot in the  street and civilians reduced the  starvation the second war lasted nine  months  [Music]  grosney was declared liberated on  february 6 2000.  for the second time the capital had  become a crumbling city journalist anna  politkovskaya described it as a  monstrous conglomerate of burnt houses  ruins and graves but with some 80 000  federal soldiers still in the country  and the kremlin maintaining much tighter  control over the state media the second  war with more than 5 000 russian  soldiers killed didn't shock the russian  public opinion as much as the first  conflict then when moscow announced in  may 2000 that it would take direct  control of chechnya putin appointed  ahmad khadirov as interim head of the  local government khadirov was one of the  most prominent former rebels who had  deserted the independence coast to take  an oath to russia kadyrov had been a  major separatist during the first  chechen war but he had always criticized  the wahhabi extremism adopted by the new  generation of soldiers so much so that  he was viewed with suspicion by his  younger colleagues for this reason in  1991 when the war broke out he and his  son ramzan didn't think twice about  siding with a federal government in  moscow the two would have brought to  their side a large group of disorders  who then formed a paramilitary force  that still exists today the cadillopsi  the followers of kadyrov those soldiers  that today we see in the military  parades organized by ramzan or part of  this group cornered by this strange new  alliance the rebels began to adopt  hardline terrorist tactics in 2002 about  40 of them broke into the dubrov  cathedral in moscow taking 850 people as  of stage  after two days of unsuccessful  negotiations the russian spatznets  released a narcotic gas into the  building which not only neutralized the  attackers but also killed 117 hostages  two years later it was the turn of a  small town in north ossetia beslan on  the first day of the new school year  more than 1 000 people many of them  children were taken hostage by the  church and separatists on the third day  of siege the building was stormed by  russian forces and about 300 people lost  their lives in the clashes  [Music]  commenting on the tragedy of aslan putin  said we showed weakness and then weak  people paid the consequences  mizlan exacerbated the war against the  chechens in march 2003 the duma issued a  new constitution in which the chechen  republic was explicitly defined as an  integral part of the russian federation  and a few months later khadirov swore in  as the new president in the meantime the  rebel movement had become a shadow of  its former self leaders were killed one  after the other and the ranks of the  spartists had been considerably reduced  in the last years of the war and with it  the hope for a free chechnya khadirova  senior and khadirov jr took the place of  putin's federal forces and that was  enough to keep the situation under  control and with time the war ended  quietly without great upheaval when in  2007 ramzan khadirov replaced this  father who died following an explosion  claimed by shamil basayev large scale  operations were long over katab who  survived the mine explosion and a  machine gun blast in the stomach had  already died in march 2002 when an  undercover fsb agent delivered him a  letter soaked in nerve agent in march  2005 mascardov by then a general without  an army had been assassinated by the  federal forces in the town of tolstoy  yurt doku umarov who took the poison  chalice as new head of the resistant  movement in 2006 declared the formation  of the kokusu's emirate with the aim of  unifying the nationalist and jihadist  movements into one common organization  but this had no concrete results and he  too in 2014 died under still unclear  circumstances probably also from  poisoning poison is a very popular  weapon for the russians it wasn't until  2009 that the russian national  anti-terrorism committee declared the  military operation in chechnya over and  determined that the conditions for  normalizing social and political life in  the republic were in place the remaining  rebels were estimated to be 500  individuals all in the countryside  according to some they are still sleeper  cells but as long as kadyrov manages the  kremlin power from a distance it's  likely that no one will make serious  claims for his part kadirov is an  outspoken supporter of vladimir putin  who in turn has repaid his support by  making him a hero brusher khadirov  enjoys nowadays a level of freedom that  no other local leader in russia enjoys  in the name of his father ahmad a huge  mosque with 4 minards has been built in  a capital and a 435 meter skyscraper is  under construction  the akhmat tower project will consist of  a 435 meter high building with 102  levels above ground and an underground  level for parking planned with 1 billion  dollars we don't know who donated this  sum the motto is akhmat is power but we  will talk about kadyrov and his  disturbing social life maybe in another  video  [Music]  for the moment one thing is certain just  as the prospect of a russia without  putin would unmask the political vacuum  that could be created in the federation  the possibility of a chechnya without  khadirov risks turning time back 20  years ago and here the question arises  in the future will the dreams of  independence be stronger than the wide  autonomy that russians have granted to  the chechens in the meantime i thank you  for keeping me company until the end i  see you in the next video ciao

