rong disinformation campaign and  armed attacks in the capital with the  first signs of chechen resistance on  december 1994 yeltsin was forced to  mobilize the army to restore what he  called the federal order it was the  beginning of the first chechen war a war  that would see one of the harshest  bombings since the one in dresden in  1945 and a war in which the russians  would experience at first hand a famous  caucasian motto that to defeat the  chechen you need another chechen on  paper the russians had all the  advantages on their side starting with  an army of more than 2 million men an  army that however was formed by quite  obsolete means and by soldiers who since  the end of the war in afghanistan hadn't  received an up-to-date training as a  consequence the russian infantry found  itself displaced when faced with a type  of combat to be adopted in chechnya on  one side the high-intensity close combat  in the urban spaces of grozny and on the  other side the lack of effective command  only left room for improvisation not all  of them proved to be incapable of course  among those who stood out were the  spatznets the russian elite corpse and  the vdv the airborne troops the russians  assumed that the capture of grozny would  mean the end of the war but the chechens  anticipated the moves placing three  defensive rings around the city and  turning it into a fortified nest with  land attacks being repelled by the  chechens who had no problems using  civilians as human shields the russians  increased their artillery attacks on the  city eventually devastating it with  bombing raids within a month what  remains of the capital fell into their  hands the fellow grozny was followed by  the flight of chechen soldiers into the  mountains and the discovery of thousands  of bodies estimated to be more than 35  000. the usce would describe that  bloodbath as an unthinkable catastrophe  when in april 1996 the head of the  chechen republic the diet was  intercepted by the russians and killed  with the guided missile the resistance  passed into the hands of his successor  another secessionist general azlan  mashadov the first thing mashadov did  was to launch a second assault on grozny  this time occupied by the russians this  kinda reminds me when a thief enters the  store only to be locked from the outside  by the owner with several thousand  russian soldiers ending up has hostages  of the chechen forces machado managed to  negotiate a temporary peace agreement  signed directly in dagestan in kazaburt  that agreement marked the momentary  victory for the about fifteen thousand  separatists but the balance of the war  was already devastating over one hundred  thousand dead nearly ten percent of the  chechen population while nearly two  hundred thousand of chechens were  refugees in afghanistan in essence in  the span of just two years more than a  third of the chechen population was  either six feet under or displaced the  regional economy was devastated and the  country had become a no man's land and  peace didn't mean friendship and only  served to freeze the church and  sovereignty the russians refused to pay  for war operations as well as for the  revenues from the passage of oil and gas  pipelines on the contrary they started  the project of another oil pipeline to  bypass chechnya altogether the baku  novoroysk pipeline this fluctuating  situation favored the development of two  phenomena in the country the arrival of  radical sunny islam of wahhabi  inspiration and the increase in  organized crime the radicalization was  fostered by the flow of money coming  straight from saudi arabia while the  local underworld was supported by the  contacts that the chechen warlords had  with the russian mafia all of this  generated the dynamite box ready to  explode in three years between 1996 and  1999 more than 1 300 kidnappings of  politicians ambassadors and foreigners  were recorded and things became more  complicated when in the mid 1990s more  chechens who had fled abroad in the  previous decades began to return from  the middle east some of this especially  those from jordan became the links  between locals and middle eastern  salafist extremists these invited saudi  and al-qaeda-affiliated commander emir  katab to chechnya qatab had fought as a  warlord against the soviets in  afghanistan where among others he had  been around bin laden circles in 1995 he  entered chechnya pretending to be a  journalist and began training chechens  by distributing funding and weapons  supplied by al-qaeda through the saudis  katsab for his part made no effort to  conceal his real goal which wasn't  chechan independence but to raise a  general jihad throughout the north  caucasus drive out christian russia and  create an islamic caliphate all it  needed was a spark qatar along with his  independentist colleague shamir basayev  found this park in april 1999 in  neighboring dagestan where a certain  bagudin magomedov proclaimed himself  emir and declared jihad for the  liberation of the caucasus and the birth  of the islamic state of dagestan  [Music]  this pissed off the russians  indeed 1999 russia was no longer the  drunken rush of yeltsin and putin was  the first that needed a higher profile  triumph  chechnya seemed an excellent opportunity  to flex his muscle and empower the  government putin also wanted to  indirectly warn the nato bloc with that  intervention implying one thing that the  caucasus was still a russian affair  however the russian intervention in  dagestan was the pretext for the let us  say controversial explosion of a series  of bombs that tore apart some apartment  buildings in three cities moscow bouinax  and bolgodonsk according to sami could  have been a provocation organized by the  russian security agencies themselves  also because a similar device linked to  the russian secret services the fsb was  found by chance in the city of ryazan  the event was used by the kremlin to  declare war a second time on the  illegitimate chechen government the  support of the russian population  obviously traumatized by the attacks on  their borders was very wide instead of  the reckless frontal assault that had  characterized the first war this time  the russian strategy was based on a well  organized and methodical plan russia  sent 30 000 soldiers to the northern  borders of chechnya starting a  stranglehold from three points north  east and west in a few weeks another  twenty thousand men joined the  sprinter-like maneuver arriving at the  end of october at the gates of grozny  with the difference that this time the  internal areas of the country had been  completely neutralized in the first war  in fact the objective had been  exclusively the capital and to do so the  various warlords had been left in  control of the countryside between mass  graves filled with corpses men suspected  of being rebels and then shot in the  street and civilians reduced the  starvation the second war lasted nine  months  [Music]  grosney was declared liberated on  february 6 2000.  for the second time the capital had  become a crumbling city journalist anna  politkovskaya described it as a  monstrous conglomerate of burnt houses  ruins and graves but with some 80 000  federal soldiers still in the country  and the kremlin maintaining much tighter  control over the state media the second  war with more than 5 000 russian  soldiers killed didn't shock the russian  public opinion as much as the first  conflict then when moscow announced in  may 2000 that it would take direct  control of chechnya putin appointed  ahmad khadirov as interim head of the  local government khadirov was one of the  most prominent former rebels who had  deserted the independence coast to take  an oath to russia kadyrov had been a  major separatist during the first  chechen war but he had always criticized  the wahhabi extremism adopted by the new  generation of soldiers so much so that  he was viewed with suspicion by his  younger colleagues for this reason in  1991 when the war broke out he and his  son ramzan didn't think twice about  siding with a federal government in  moscow the two would have brought to  their side a large group of disorders  who then formed a paramilitary force  that still exists today the cadillopsi  the followers of kadyrov those soldiers  that today we see in the military  parades organized by ramzan or part of  this group cornered by this strange new  alliance the rebels began to adopt  hardline terrorist tactics in 2002 about  40 of them broke into the dubrov  cathedral in moscow taking 850 people as  of stage  after two days of unsuccessful  negotiations the russian spatznets  released a narcotic gas into the  building which not only neutralized the  attackers but also killed 117 hostages  two years later it was the turn of a  small town in north ossetia beslan on  the first day of the new school year  more than 1 000 people many of them  children were taken hostage by the  church and separatists on the third day  of siege the building was stormed by  russian forces and about 300 people lost  their lives in the clashes  [Music]  commenting on the tragedy of aslan putin  said we showed weakness and then weak  people paid the consequences  mizlan exacerbated the war against the  chechens in march 2003 the duma issued a  new constitution in which the chechen  republic was explicitly defined as an  integral part of the russian federation  and a few months later khadirov swore in  as the new president in the meantime the  rebel movement had become a shadow of  its former self leaders were killed one  after the other and the ranks of the  spartists had been considerably reduced  in the last years of the war and with it  the hope for a free chechnya khadirova  senior and khadirov jr took the place of  putin's federal forces and that was  enough to keep the situation under  control and with time the war ended  quietly without great upheaval when in  2007 ramzan khadirov replaced this  father who died following an explosion  claimed by shamil basayev large scale  operations were long over katab who  survived the mine explosion and a  machine gun blast in the stomach had  already died in march 2002 when an  undercover fsb agent delivered him a  letter soaked in nerve agent in march  2005 mascardov by then a general without  an army had been assassinated by the  federal forces in the town of tolstoy  yurt doku umarov who took the poison  chalice as new head of the resistant  movement in 2006 declared the formation  of the kokusu's emirate with the aim of  unifying the nationalist and jihadist  movements into one common organization  but this had no concrete results and he  too in 2014 died under still unclear  circumstances probably also from  poisoning poison is a very popular  weapon for the russians it wasn't until  2009 that the russian national  anti-terrorism committee declared the  military operation in chechnya over and  determined that the conditions for  normalizing social and political life in  the republic were in place the remaining  rebels were estimated to be 500  individuals all in the countryside  according to some they are still sleeper  cells but as long as kadyrov manages the  kremlin power from a distance it's  likely that no one will make serious  claims for his part kadirov is an  outspoken supporter of vladimir putin  who in turn has repaid his support by  making him a hero brusher khadirov  enjoys nowadays a level of freedom that  no other local leader in russia enjoys  in the name of his father ahmad a huge  mosque with 4 minards has been built in  a capital and a 435 meter skyscraper is  under construction  the akhmat tower project will consist of  a 435 meter high building with 102  levels above ground and an underground  level for parking planned with 1 billion  dollars we don't know who donated this  sum the motto is akhmat is power but we  will talk about kadyrov and his  disturbing social life maybe in another  video  [Music]  for the moment one thing is certain just  as the prospect of a russia without  putin would unmask the political vacuum  that could be created in the federation  the possibility of a chechnya without  khadirov risks turning time back 20  years ago and here the question arises  in the future will the dreams of  independence be stronger than the wide  autonomy that russians have granted to  the chechens in the meantime i thank you  for keeping me company until the end i  see you in the next video ciao
 

